  "question": "How does Manton describe the two kinds of mercy God extends and the way each is conveyed?",
  "answer": "Manton explains that God dispenses two distinct mercies: a common mercy that reaches every creature and a saving mercy reserved for God’s peculiar people. He distinguishes them by their subjects and their means of conveyance, noting that the common mercy \"the one floweth in the channel of common providence, the other is conveyed to us by the golden pipe of the Mediator.\" Thus, while all created beings benefit from God’s general goodness, the elect receive a special, mediatorial channel of grace."
}

{
  "question": "How does Witsius describe the change in a believer’s understanding and will when they submit to the Holy Spirit?",
  "answer": "Witsius teaches that once the understanding and will are properly ordered, the chaotic passions are gradually subdued and the believer learns to wait for reason’s commands before acting. In this state the soul \"calmly resign[s] themselves to be governed by the Holy Spirit, receiving from him, with full submission, the law of motion and of rest.\" The former fury of lust is replaced by a higher, heavenly orientation, and the mind is driven toward objects that are \"holy, heavenly, and becoming a Christian\" while being enjoined to a perfect silent submission to God’s decree and precept. [Systematic — Witsius Economy Of The Covenants Vol2]"
}

{
  "question": "What does the Greenham work say about the importance of God’s promises for believers facing afflictions?",
  "answer": "The Greenham work teaches that in every affliction believers must ‘crave of God, that we may not use unfull meanes, but rather the promises of God, as our chiefest strength, & feeling them with Gods fauour in vs’, so that their hearts remain fixed on those promises. By doing so, ‘the Lord giue successe and blessing to na turall and secondary meanes’. The passage emphasizes that reliance on divine promises, rather than worldly or secondary means, is the chief source of strength and leads to divine blessing even amid trials. [Greenham — Works]"
}

{
  "question": "What two spiritual facts did the prodigal realize according to the sermon?",
  "answer": "Spurgeon teaches that when the prodigal came to himself he was shut up to two thoughts. The two facts clear to him were that there was plenty in his father’s house, and that he himself was famishing. The sermon also records the divine Physician’s promise, \"I will; be thou made whole.\" [Sermon 1000 — \"Number One Thousand; Or, \\\"Bread Enough and To Spare\\\"\"]"
}

{
  "question": "According to Goodwin, how does the relationship between believers and Christ relate to the concept of marriage and heavenly blessings?",
  "answer": "Goodwin teaches that believers are described as the spouse of Christ, so that they share the same blessings and location as their Lord. He writes that \"the spouse must be where the husband is,\" meaning that as Christ is a heavenly man, those united to Him are likewise raised to heavenly places and receive heavenly blessings, not merely spiritual benefits. This marital metaphor underscores that believers, being married to Christ, are taken to His Father's house, heaven, and partake of the same heavenly inheritance. [Goodwin — Works Vol01]"
}

{
  "question": "According to Owen in the work titled “Indwelling Sin,” what does he identify as the true cause of a person’s guilt when they sin, and how does he refute the claim that God tempts people to sin?",
  "answer": "Owen argues that the guilt of sin rests entirely on the individual’s own lust and inclination, not on external temptations or on God. He cites the scriptural principle, quoting James, “Every man is tempted when he is drawn away of his own luit, and en¬ ticed, Then, when luit hath conceived, it bring- eth forth fin : and fin, when it is Unified, bring- eth forth death,” to show that sin originates from the person’s internal desire. He then rebukes the excuse that “Let no man fay, when he is tempted, I am tempted of God ; tor God cannot be tempted of evil, neither tempteth he any rman/’ ver,” emphasizing that God cannot be the source of evil temptation. Consequently, Owen concludes that the whole guilt and subsequent punishment belong to the sinner unless grace intervenes to prevent it. [Owen — Indwelling Sin]"
}

{
  "question": "What does Flavel describe as the most miserable circumstance for a virtuous person?",
  "answer": "Flavel teaches that the greatest misery is to see a virtuous and worthy person exposed to the sea, writing that \"is more miserable, than to see a Virtuous and | Worthy Person upon the Sea.\" He argues that the perils of the ocean endanger the soul of even the most pious, making such a sight a profound source of sorrow. [Flavel — Works Vol02]"
}

{
  "question": "What does Sibbes mean by 'tempting the Spirit' in his discussion of believers resisting the Holy Spirit?",
  "answer": "Sibbes explains that to tempt the Spirit is to willfully test God's forgiveness by committing sinful acts and seeing whether He will 'put it off'. He illustrates this with the example, \"Ananias and Sapphira tempted the Spirit, Acts v. 9 — that is, when men will do that which is naught, and try whether God will forgive them, and put it off\\\"\", showing that such resistance quenches the Spirit’s work in the soul. [Sibbes — Works Vol01]"
}

{
  "question": "How does Shedd differentiate between immediate and mediate imputation in his dogmatic theology?",
  "answer": "Shedd distinguishes immediate imputation from mediate imputation, noting a clear doctrinal separation. He records the \"difference between immediate and mediate im- putation, ii. 193 ; reason for the former, and objection to the latter, ii. 192-194\" and explains that the former is grounded in a direct attribution of Adam's guilt to all humanity, whereas the latter involves a secondary, mediated reasoning that he objects to. This systematic treatment reflects his careful analysis of the doctrine. [Systematic — Shedd Dogmatic Theology Vol2]"
}

{
  "question": "What does Gill say about the inward (soul) aspect of temporal punishment for sin?",
  "answer": "Gill teaches that the inward punishment of sin consists in a loss of the divine image and righteousness. He explains that this loss is manifested as \"In a loss of the image of God upon it; all have sinned and \\\"come short\\\", or \\\"are deprived of the glory of God\\\"\" and further notes that because of this loss \"There is none righteous, no not one\" (Rom. 3:10, 23). [Systematic — Gill Body Of Doctrinal Divinity]"
}
